/-!
# `Work.K3Inputs.HLItems` — items (1)–(4) of Theorem `thm-HL`, as named hypothesis-level Props

Lin–Shinder, *Motivic invariants of birational maps*, Annals **199** (1), 2024, § *K3 surfaces of
degree 12*, Theorem `thm-HL` (tex 2223–2280), quoting Hassett–Lai Theorem 2.1 and Theorem 3.1:

> Let `(S,H)` be a general polarized complex K3 surface with `H² = 12` and let
> `Σ = {x₁,x₂,x₃} ⊂ S` be a general triple of points.  The linear system `|H|` defines an
> embedding `S ↪ ℙ⁷`; let `S₀ ⊂ ℙ⁴` be the proper transform of `S` under the projection
> `ℙ⁷ ⤏ ℙ⁴` from the plane generated by `Σ`.  We have the following:
> 1. `S₀` has three transverse double points, and the normalization of `S₀` is `Bl_Σ S`.
> 2. The linear system of quartics containing `S₀` cuts out `S₀` as a scheme, and defines a
>    birational map `ψ : ℙ⁴ ⤏ ℙ⁴`.
> 3. The inverse `ψ⁻¹ : ℙ⁴ ⤏ ℙ⁴` is constructed the same way, starting from another K3 surface
>    `S'` of degree `12` and three points `Σ' = {x'₁,x'₂,x'₃}` on `S'`.
> 4. We have `Bl_{S₀}ℙ⁴ ≃ Bl_{S'₀}ℙ⁴` and it resolves `ψ`.
>
> Furthermore, if `Pic(S) = ℤ·H`, then `S'` is the unique Fourier–Mukai partner of `S` such that
> `S ≄ S'`.  Finally, if `S ↪ ℙ⁷` and `Σ ⊂ S` are defined over a subfield `k ⊂ ℂ`, then both `ψ`
> and the pair `(S',Σ')` are defined over `k`.

None of the objects in items (1)–(4) exists in Mathlib: there is no projective space `ℙⁿ`, no
Grassmannian `OG₊(5,10)`, no blow-up of a scheme along a closed subscheme, no normalization, no
linear system of quartics, no minimal model, no Fourier–Mukai partner.  Following the discipline
of the sibling areas (`Submission/Work/CrauderKatz`, `Submission/Work/LLink`), each item is
recorded here as a **named hypothesis-level `Prop`** in the K3 chapter's vocabulary, and the
*provable* content around them is proved:

* `Work.K3Inputs.LinkCore.bridge` — the ℙ¹-bundle bridge `bridge` of `Work.K3.K3Data` is derived
  from (F1) (the exceptional divisors are `ℙ¹`-bundles over the centres, item (1) of `thm-HL`)
  and (F2) (the cylinder rigidity `lem-stbirCY`);
* `Work.K3Inputs.HLInput.bridge_bc` — the same, uniformly over every subfield `𝕜 ⊆ ℂ`, which is
  the clause `bridge_bc` of `Work.K3.HassettLaiTheorem`;
* `Work.K3Inputs.HLInput.toHassettLaiTheorem` — the assembly: the K3 chapter's geometric input
  `Work.K3.HassettLaiTheorem T`, with **both** bridges proved, from the item-level clauses.

The last sentence of `thm-HL` (defined over `k`) is the descent step, which is *proved* in
`Submission/Work/K3/Uniform.lean` (`Work.K3.HassettLaiTheorem.toHLDescent`); the item below
records only its residue on the input side, namely that the whole construction is carried out
over `ℚ` — which is why the input `Work.K3.TerasomaInput` is stated over `ℚ`.
-/

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option linter.unusedSectionVars false
set_option linter.style.haveILetI false
set_option warn.classDefReducibility false
attribute [instance] MotivicInvariants.Variety.over MotivicInvariants.Variety.isIntegral
attribute [instance] Work.K3.K3Data.varietyS Work.K3.K3Data.varietyS' Work.K3.K3Data.varietyP
attribute [instance] Work.K3.TerasomaInput.varietyS Work.K3.TerasomaInput.overSigma
  Work.K3.TerasomaInput.isOverSigma

namespace Work.K3Inputs

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits AlgebraicGeometry AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme
  MotivicInvariants Order

noncomputable section

/-! ## 1. The four items of `thm-HL` as named Props -/

/-- **Item (1) of Theorem `thm-HL`.**  *"`S₀` has three transverse double points, and the
normalization of `S₀` is `Bl_Σ S`."*

`S` is the K3 surface of degree `12`, `σ : Σ ⟶ S` the length-three subscheme, `ι₀ : S₀ ⟶ P` the
proper transform of `S` in the model `P` of `ℙ⁴` (under the projection `ℙ⁷ ⤏ ℙ⁴` from the
`Σ`-plane).  The *named* content is: (i) the singular locus of `S₀` consists of exactly three
transverse (ordinary) double points; (ii) the normalization of `S₀` is the blow-up of `S` in the
three points `Σ`.  None of this is expressible in Mathlib (no normalizations, no blow-ups, no
`ℙ⁷`, no linear projections), so this is a named hypothesis. -/
class HasThreeTransverseDoublePointsAndNormalisation (𝕜 : Type) [Field 𝕜]
    {S Sig S0 P : Scheme.{0}} [Variety 𝕜 S] [Sig.Over (Spec (.of 𝕜))] [S0.Over (Spec (.of 𝕜))]
    [P.Over (Spec (.of 𝕜))] (σ : Sig ⟶ S) (ι0 : S0 ⟶ P) : Prop

/-- **Item (2) of Theorem `thm-HL`.**  *"The linear system of quartics containing `S₀` cuts out
`S₀` as a scheme, and defines a birational map `ψ : ℙ⁴ ⤏ ℙ⁴`."*

The named content: the base locus of the (5-dimensional) linear system `|𝒪_{ℙ⁴}(4) ⊗ I_{S₀}|` is
`S₀` scheme-theoretically, and the resulting rational map `ψ` (given by the linear system) is
birational.  Mathlib has no linear systems on projective space and no `ℙ⁴`; the *output* `ψ` is
part of the link data
(`Work.K3Inputs.LinkCore.ψ`), so what is named here is the relation between `ι₀` and `ψ`. -/
class QuarticSystemDefinesBirational (𝕜 : Type) [Field 𝕜] {S0 P : Scheme.{0}}
    [S0.Over (Spec (.of 𝕜))] [P.Over (Spec (.of 𝕜))] [IrreducibleSpace P] (ι0 : S0 ⟶ P)
    (ψ : P.BirationalMap P) : Prop

/-- **Item (3) of Theorem `thm-HL`.**  *"The inverse `ψ⁻¹ : ℙ⁴ ⤏ ℙ⁴` is constructed the same way,
starting from another K3 surface `S'` of degree `12` and three points `Σ' = {x'₁,x'₂,x'₃}` on
`S'`."*

`ι₀' : S₀' ⟶ P` is the proper transform of `S'` under the other projection, `σ' : Σ' ⟶ S'` the
length-three subscheme; the named content is that the *inverse* of `ψ` arises from `(S',Σ')` by
the same quartic-system construction as in item (2) applied to `ι₀'`. -/
class InverseConstructedFromPartner (𝕜 : Type) [Field 𝕜] {S0 S0' P S' Sig' : Scheme.{0}}
    [S0.Over (Spec (.of 𝕜))] [S0'.Over (Spec (.of 𝕜))] [P.Over (Spec (.of 𝕜))]
    [IrreducibleSpace P] [Variety 𝕜 S']
    [Sig'.Over (Spec (.of 𝕜))] (ι0 : S0 ⟶ P) (ι0' : S0' ⟶ P) (σ' : Sig' ⟶ S')
    (ψ : P.BirationalMap P) : Prop

/-- **Item (4) of Theorem `thm-HL`.**  *"We have `Bl_{S₀}ℙ⁴ ≃ Bl_{S'₀}ℙ⁴` and it resolves
`ψ`."*

`T` is the common blow-up, `bl : T ⟶ P` and `bl' : T ⟶ P` the two blow-down maps (of `S₀` and
of `S₀'` respectively, in the same `P`).  The named content: (i) `T` is the blow-up of `P` in `S₀`
and, via the other map, in `S₀'`; (ii) the rational map `ψ` is resolved by `T` in both
directions.  This is the "explicit resolution machinery" of the paper; Mathlib has no blow-ups of
schemes. -/
class BlowUpIdentification (𝕜 : Type) [Field 𝕜] {P S0 S0' T : Scheme.{0}}
    [P.Over (Spec (.of 𝕜))] [IrreducibleSpace P] [S0.Over (Spec (.of 𝕜))]
    [S0'.Over (Spec (.of 𝕜))]
    [T.Over (Spec (.of 𝕜))] (ι0 : S0 ⟶ P) (ι0' : S0' ⟶ P) (ψ : P.BirationalMap P)
    (bl : T ⟶ P) (bl' : T ⟶ P) : Prop

/-- **The Fourier–Mukai clause of Theorem `thm-HL`.**  *"Furthermore, if `Pic(S) = ℤ·H`, then `S'`
is the unique Fourier–Mukai partner of `S` such that `S ≄ S'`."*

Named: Mathlib has no derived category of coherent sheaves, no Fourier–Mukai transforms and no
K3 surfaces.  The *consequence* consumed by the K3 chapter is `notIsoGeom` (`S_ℂ ≄ S'_ℂ`), a field
of `Work.K3.HassettLaiTheorem`; this predicate records the stronger statement that `S'` is the
*unique* partner other than `S` itself, which is how `thm:K3`'s "distinct" clause is obtained
(Hassett–Lai Thm 3.1 + Oguiso Prop 1.10). -/
class IsUniqueFourierMukaiPartner (𝕜 : Type) [Field 𝕜] (S S' : Scheme.{0}) [Variety 𝕜 S]
    [Variety 𝕜 S'] : Prop

/-! ## 2. The input structure: `HassettLaiTheorem` with both bridges proved -/

/-- **The Hassett–Lai input, with the two bridge clauses proved.**

Every field of `Work.K3.HassettLaiTheorem T` is present, except that

* the link data is a `Work.K3Inputs.LinkCore ℚ` — the same data with the ℙ¹-bundle bridge
  `bridge` replaced by the geometric clauses (F1) `y_cylinder`, `x_cylinder` and (F2)
  `cylinderRigid` (`LinkCore.bridge` proves `data.bridge` from them), and
* the uniform bridge clause `bridge_bc` is replaced by its three geometric consequences
  `y_cylinder_bc`, `x_cylinder_bc`, `cylinderRigid_bc` (`HLInput.bridge_bc` proves it from them).

`HLInput.toHassettLaiTheorem` assembles `Work.K3.HassettLaiTheorem T` from this — so the residual
assumptions of the K3 chapter are exactly the four items of `thm-HL` (in their named form above)
together with the geometric clauses (F1)/(F2) over each subfield. -/
structure HLInput (T : Work.K3.TerasomaInput) where
  /-- the `ℚ`-model of the L-link, with the bridge replaced by the clauses (F1), (F2) -/
  core : LinkCore ℚ
  /-- the first centre of the link is the input surface `T.S`, up to `ℚ`-isomorphism -/
  centre : Work.K3.IsoOver ℚ T.S core.S
  /-- the length-three subscheme `Σ' ⊂ S'` from which `ψ⁻¹` is constructed -/
  Sigma' : Scheme.{0}
  [overSigma' : Sigma'.Over (Spec (.of ℚ))]
  /-- the closed immersion `Σ' ⟶ S'` -/
  sigma' : Sigma' ⟶ core.S'
  [isOverSigma' : sigma'.IsOver (Spec (.of ℚ))]
  [isClosedImmersionSigma' : IsClosedImmersion sigma']
  [isReducedSigma' : IsReduced Sigma']
  [smoothSigma' : Smooth sigma']
  /-- `Σ'` has length three (named predicate). -/
  hasLengthThree' : Work.K3.HasLengthThree ℚ sigma'
  /-- `(S', Σ')` is again HL-admissible. -/
  hl' : Work.K3.HLAdmissible ℚ sigma'
  /-- `S_ℂ ≄ S'_ℂ` (the Fourier–Mukai clause; see `IsUniqueFourierMukaiPartner`). -/
  notIsoGeom : ¬ Work.K3.IsoOver ℂ (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ℂ core.S)
    (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ℂ core.S')
  /- ### Base-change-uniform clauses (verbatim from `Work.K3.HassettLaiTheorem`) -/
  /-- `S` is geometrically integral. -/
  [geomIntS : GeometricallyIntegral (core.S ↘ Spec (.of ℚ))]
  /-- `S'` is geometrically integral. -/
  [geomIntS' : GeometricallyIntegral (core.S' ↘ Spec (.of ℚ))]
  /-- the fourfold `P` is geometrically integral. -/
  [geomIntP : GeometricallyIntegral (core.P ↘ Spec (.of ℚ))]
  /-- `S_𝕜` is again a K3 surface of degree `12`. -/
  isK3_bc : ∀ (𝕜 : Subfield ℂ) [Variety ↥𝕜 (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.S)],
    Work.K3.IsK3Degree12 ↥𝕜 (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.S)
  /-- `S_𝕜` is `K`-trivial. -/
  kTrivial_bc : ∀ (𝕜 : Subfield ℂ) [Variety ↥𝕜 (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.S)],
    Work.K3.IsKTrivial ↥𝕜 (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.S)
  /-- `S_𝕜` has geometric Picard rank one. -/
  picardOne_bc : ∀ (𝕜 : Subfield ℂ) [Variety ↥𝕜 (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.S)],
    Work.K3.PicardRankOneGeom ↥𝕜 (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.S)
  /-- `S'_𝕜` is again a K3 surface of degree `12`. -/
  isK3'_bc : ∀ (𝕜 : Subfield ℂ) [Variety ↥𝕜 (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.S')],
    Work.K3.IsK3Degree12 ↥𝕜 (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.S')
  /-- `S'_𝕜` is `K`-trivial. -/
  kTrivial'_bc : ∀ (𝕜 : Subfield ℂ) [Variety ↥𝕜 (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.S')],
    Work.K3.IsKTrivial ↥𝕜 (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.S')
  /-- `S'_𝕜` has geometric Picard rank one. -/
  picardOne'_bc : ∀ (𝕜 : Subfield ℂ) [Variety ↥𝕜 (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.S')],
    Work.K3.PicardRankOneGeom ↥𝕜 (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.S')
  /-- the length-three subscheme `Σ'` base changes to a length-three subscheme. -/
  hasLengthThree_bc : ∀ (𝕜 : Subfield ℂ),
    Work.K3.HasLengthThree ↥𝕜 (Work.K3.baseChangeMap ℚ ↥𝕜 sigma')
  /-- the HL-admissible pair `(S', Σ')` base changes to an HL-admissible pair. -/
  hl_bc : ∀ (𝕜 : Subfield ℂ), Work.K3.HLAdmissible ↥𝕜 (Work.K3.baseChangeMap ℚ ↥𝕜 sigma')
  /-- **(L3) transported**: the divisor `x` is geometrically irreducible. -/
  x_codim1 : ∀ (𝕜 : Subfield ℂ), ∃! p : Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.P,
    Work.K3.baseChangeProj ℚ ↥𝕜 core.P p = core.x ∧ coheight p = 1
  /-- **(L3) transported**: the divisor `y` is geometrically irreducible. -/
  y_codim1 : ∀ (𝕜 : Subfield ℂ), ∃! p : Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.P,
    Work.K3.baseChangeProj ℚ ↥𝕜 core.P p = core.y ∧ coheight p = 1
  /-- the non-isomorphism locus of `ψ` base changes. -/
  x_notIso : ∀ (𝕜 : Subfield ℂ) [IrreducibleSpace (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.P)]
    (Ψ : (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.P).BirationalMap (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.P)),
    (∀ (g : core.P.PartialMap core.P) [g.IsOver (Spec (.of ℚ))], g.toRationalMap = core.ψ.hom →
      (Work.K3.PartialMap.baseChangeAlong (Work.K3.specMap ℚ ↥𝕜) g).toRationalMap = Ψ.hom) →
    ∀ p : Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.P, Work.K3.baseChangeProj ℚ ↥𝕜 core.P p = core.x →
      ¬ IsIsoAt Ψ p
  /-- the non-isomorphism locus of `ψ⁻¹` base changes. -/
  y_notIso : ∀ (𝕜 : Subfield ℂ) [IrreducibleSpace (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.P)]
    (Ψ : (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.P).BirationalMap (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.P)),
    (∀ (g : core.P.PartialMap core.P) [g.IsOver (Spec (.of ℚ))], g.toRationalMap = core.ψ.inv →
      (Work.K3.PartialMap.baseChangeAlong (Work.K3.specMap ℚ ↥𝕜) g).toRationalMap = Ψ.hom) →
    ∀ p : Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.P, Work.K3.baseChangeProj ℚ ↥𝕜 core.P p = core.y →
      ¬ IsIsoAt Ψ p
  /- ### The clauses replacing `bridge_bc`: (F1) and (F2) over each subfield -/
  /-- **(F1) base-change-uniform**: the (unique) codimension-one point of `P_𝕜` over `y` has
  function field `κ(S_𝕜)(t)`.

  The `Over`-structure of the base-changed objects is written explicitly, through
  `Work.K3.baseChangeOver`: the ambient typeclass search has two candidates for
  `(baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 X).Over (Spec ↥𝕜)` (this one and `MotivicInvariants.Variety.over` of a
  `Variety` argument), and fixing the instance keeps the clause's type stable. -/
  y_cylinder_bc : ∀ (𝕜 : Subfield ℂ) [IsIntegral (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.S)]
    (q : Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.P),
    Work.K3.baseChangeProj ℚ ↥𝕜 core.P q = core.y → coheight q = 1 →
    @IsP1BundleOver ↥𝕜 _ (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.P)
      (Work.K3.baseChangeOver ℚ ↥𝕜 core.P) q (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.S) _
      (Work.K3.baseChangeOver ℚ ↥𝕜 core.S)
  /-- **(F1) base-change-uniform**, for the other exceptional divisor. -/
  x_cylinder_bc : ∀ (𝕜 : Subfield ℂ) [IsIntegral (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.S')]
    (p : Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.P),
    Work.K3.baseChangeProj ℚ ↥𝕜 core.P p = core.x → coheight p = 1 →
    @IsP1BundleOver ↥𝕜 _ (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.P)
      (Work.K3.baseChangeOver ℚ ↥𝕜 core.P) p (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.S') _
      (Work.K3.baseChangeOver ℚ ↥𝕜 core.S')
  /-- **(F2) base-change-uniform**: the cylinder rigidity of the centres over each subfield. -/
  cylinderRigid_bc : ∀ (𝕜 : Subfield ℂ) [IsIntegral (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.S)]
    [IsIntegral (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.S')],
    @CylinderRigid ↥𝕜 _ (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.S) (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.S')
      _ _ (Work.K3.baseChangeOver ℚ ↥𝕜 core.S) (Work.K3.baseChangeOver ℚ ↥𝕜 core.S')
  /-- strong rationality base changes: the transports `ε_𝕜`, `δ_𝕜` with vanishing coefficient. -/
  transports_bc : ∀ (𝕜 : Subfield ℂ) [IrreducibleSpace (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.P)],
    ∃ (ε : (Work.K3.Affine4 ↥𝕜).BirationalMap (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.P))
      (δ : (Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.P).BirationalMap (Work.K3.Affine4 ↥𝕜)),
      ε.IsOver (Spec (.of ↥𝕜)) ∧ δ.IsOver (Spec (.of ↥𝕜)) ∧
      (∀ z : Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.P, cInv ↥𝕜 z ε = 0) ∧
      (∀ z : Work.K3.baseChange ℚ ↥𝕜 core.P, cInv ↥𝕜 z δ = 0)

attribute [instance] HLInput.overSigma' HLInput.isOverSigma' HLInput.isClosedImmersionSigma'
  HLInput.isReducedSigma' HLInput.smoothSigma' HLInput.geomIntS HLInput.geomIntS'
  HLInput.geomIntP

end

end Work.K3Inputs
